WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.790
Hey, so I'm back with the fourth Ask Preethi series today and today's question is

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.269
a very, again, a very common one. This person is asking what language should I start with?

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:19.350
Should I start with--and also should I start with the front-end, back-end, machine learning, data science? I have no idea what language or where to start.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.299
Of course this is an overwhelming thing to decide when you're getting started with programming

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:30.869
particularly because there's so much to learn in computer science and programming in general that it's

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:35.699
really hard for a beginner to know where to start. And I remember when I was getting started

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.139
I kind of googled this question, and the worst part about googling is that everyone has their own answers.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.299
And they have their own opinions.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.969
And so they all seem right because they have experience, and something worked for some people, something didn't work for other people, so

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.869
It's a very good question. I struggled with it, too.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:58.050
And of course, as for everything in software engineering, I'm gonna say that it depends.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.920
Some of you might be starting off in programming in a very specific

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.720
vertical. For example, if you know you want to do machine learning

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.120
or you know you want to do data science or you know you want to do mobile

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.799
development, iOS or Android, or you know you want to do security stuff for distributed systems.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.990
If you know the vertical already

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.890
Then there's usually a few specific languages that those particular verticals

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.799
generalize on. For example, with machine learning and data science, a lot of people use Python

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:32.669
And there's a lot of machine learning and data science libraries and math-related libraries written in Python, and so

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.909
most of the data scientists I know write in Python.

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:43.169
So I would say if you're going specifically for that vertical, pick the language that that vertical is standardizing on, right? And, like,

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:47.429
why would you go learn Haskell if you're trying to do machine learning? It doesn't make sense.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.249
But let's assume that for this question

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:56.849
you are just kind of starting out new. You haven't really decided where you want to focus. You're starting from a clean slate,

00:01:57.369 --> 00:01:59.879
and you really have a choice of anything that you can do.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.939
And that's the question

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:08.038
I'll answer today, so I'm gonna be biased and say the best thing we should start with is JavaScript.

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.509
And just to give you a personal background--so I started with

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.700
learning to code my first language, which was actually not JavaScript. I started to learn Python and

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:25.410
I learned I was trying to learn it through a book called "Learn Python the Hard Way" and I tried many, many, times and I

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.280
failed.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.380
I tried--I remember the first time I stopped it after two weeks, the second time I stopped doing it after five weeks.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.049
I tried a bunch of online courses.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.050
Maybe I thought the video format would help.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.990
Something about it was just not clicking to the point where I was getting really frustrated.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.159
I was like, you know what, maybe programming is not for me. Like it just wasn't working.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.990
And then I remember I was kind of expressing the struggle with my

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.599
ex-boyfriend at the time, and he was like, "Why are you learning Python? Go learn JavaScript."

00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:01.320
I was like, "What's JavaScript?" And I came home that night, and I did a bunch of Google searching, and I found this

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.130
Codecademy course. It's like a 10-hour course or something on JavaScript

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.469
And I remember I didn't even go to work the next day

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:13.320
I just sat there and went through the whole JavaScript intro course and after that I was hooked.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.309
It was like I was unstoppable. I fell in love with JavaScript.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.640
There was something amazing about writing some JavaScript,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.809
and then along with it, some HTML and CSS and then seeing--

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.190
refreshing the browser and seeing the changes--that just made my heart beat so fast.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.490
I couldn't--I couldn't stop myself from  loving it.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:37.140
After that, I was just unstoppable. I was hooked on JavaScript, and really,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.179
Javascript was like my gateway drug to programming,

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.530
if you can think of it that way.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.320
And I think the reason why JavaScript is so beginner-friendly is because a few things. One is

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:55.440
it has that immediate feedback loop that I was talking about. So you can write something in your IDE

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.690
and then you can refresh your browser and see it right away.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.399
Whereas some other languages, like Java or Haskell, they require a compile step

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.890
where you actually need to compile the code before you can actually

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:11.249
see what happens. Which adds a little bit of burden for a beginner and kind of makes it a little bit more confusing.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:17.040
Two, there's no complex developer environments. You just set up. For example, if you're coding for iOS or Android,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:22.109
there's a whole process for setting up Xcode or the Android Studio thing and

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:26.819
That in itself is kind of daunting. Whereas with JavaScript, you can just download any

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:33.990
IDE, or basically Notepad, and code in that and be fine with it.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.769
And you don't need all these complex toolings to get started.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.229
Three, as I said before, you don't need to compile and figure that whole thing out.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.710
Another big reason is because there's no types.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.280
JavaScript is is a dynamically typed language.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.709
And that means you don't have to write types, like whether a variable is a number

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.809
or string or whatever, and that removes a huge burden on beginners,

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:06.600
I think. When I was looking at some other code, like Java code, for example, when I was getting started,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.679
it was pretty daunting to see both the code and the types. I didn't know what the difference was

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.680
between them. Whereas JavaScript,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.640
you just kind of write code

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.530
and you don't worry about the types. And that removes one extra thing to learn. Honestly, sometimes

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.049
it makes it visually easy on the eyes for beginners.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.600
I would argue that as you get more advanced

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.859
I actually like types now because it makes me--it makes it easier for me to read code.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.910
But when I was a beginner that wasn't the case. I think types added an extra burden

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.229
when I was a beginner. So JavaScript has no types, which I think is amazing

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.990
for beginners only.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:46.829
And then the other reason is because it runs everywhere. So if you write JavaScript, it runs in every browser:

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:49.110
Chrome, Firefox.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:54.180
Whatever it is, it runs on every operating system, every device, so you can--a beginner

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.739
can get started with JavaScript and write for various kinds of platforms, and that's pretty cool.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.160
And lastly, I think it's very versatile, too.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:08.039
So JavaScript you can use it as a scripting language. You can use it to build complex web applications.

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:13.679
You can use it with nodes, you can book sophisticated API backends.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.200
You can even build bots and IoT applications.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:22.470
You can write native desktop applications using Electron, you can write native mobile applications using React Native.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:26.760
All in JavaScript. You can even do VR stuff now with React VR.

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:29.579
It's just, it's pretty amazing that you can take

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:34.979
JavaScript and just like do so much with it. Whereas some other languages are not as easy to do that

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.030
and are not as transportable in that sense.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:46.649
So if you're struggling to get started, and you haven't tried JavaScript yet, I say go for JavaScript and see if you like it.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:50.339
To me, again, JavaScript was the gateway drug into programming.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:57.630
It's really how I learned to code, and it's really where I learned the fundamentals of programming, and this is things like loops,

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.810
conditionals, functions, data types, variables,

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:08.100
classes, inheritance, prototypes. Like, all these things are fundamental to almost every programming language

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.019
And I kind of learned the fundamentals of those--of a programming language--through JavaScript.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:18.329
JavaScript was kind of just the tool to get me there.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.559
And now that I know JavaScript, I can pretty much

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.290
apply that and read and

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:32.730
understand code in almost any language. So I can read Python code. I can read Go. I can read Ruby. I can read C++.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.000
I can read

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.809
Haskell. Like, I'm not able to maybe write in it as like a super expert programmer.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:45.720
But I can read and understand it pretty well because the syntax--you start to get familiar with the same types of syntax,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.409
loops, the conditionals, the functions and so forth. And

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.170
I guess, like,

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:59.250
the takeaway is the language you just--in the end, just pick the language that makes you want to code. That makes

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.519
you want to like really get into programming, and the one that you

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:03.690
specifically like the most.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:08.549
It doesn't matter what other people tell you, because what other people tell you--people recommended Python to me, like outside,

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:14.220
outside when I got started. The reason for Python is that, because other people recommended it to me,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.600
and it didn't work for me.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.739
So I had to explore other things to find the ones that work for me, and to find the one

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.079
that made me fall in love with programming.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:27.389
And then you can always expand from there. It's not like, just because you started with JavaScript

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:29.640
you have to stick with fun and you have to stick with JavaScript.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.759
Or it's not just because you started with Python, you have to stick with Python.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:38.610
And you're limited to what only Python offers. Like, why don't you learn one language that is applicable to any language?

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:43.139
And you can kind of learn new languages much more quickly. Like, I was able to learn Python

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.409
so much quicker than JavaScript, after I learned JavaScript.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:53.129
Just because it's very similar. And same with wanting to learn Python. Running, learning Ruby was such a such a breeze.

00:08:53.259 --> 00:08:57.929
Yeah, it takes a little bit longer to understand the specific details of how the syntax works.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:02.219
But once you get past that syntax riddle,  most languages are kind of the same.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.639
And I would say

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:11.549
once you actually learn the language, don't get stuck in it, either.

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:14.969
Be broad. So, well, that's one of the things I did when I was

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:20.490
kind of more of a beginner. I was like, even though I learned JavaScript

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:24.060
I was keeping my eyes open for all different things. I learned Node.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:30.690
I learned--I even start to learn other languages like Python, Ruby. I started to learn more back-end stuff. I started to learn,

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:32.649
like,

00:09:32.649 --> 00:09:39.568
things to do with Docker and more like system level stuff. I kind of just kept myself broad because I want to

00:09:40.269 --> 00:09:42.689
really be all across the stack and

00:09:43.269 --> 00:09:48.508
understand what I like best, what types of problems I like to solve the most. What communities, what programming communities

00:09:48.509 --> 00:09:50.818
I like the most, where can I be the most productive.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:55.229
And so I kept learning new languages, frameworks, technologies, and

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:02.310
through that exploration, I was able to figure out what I really want to do right now,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:06.959
which is focus on blockchain engineering only for the foreseeable future.

00:10:06.959 --> 00:10:11.759
But I wouldn't have been able to go in there if I didn't explore all these other things and figure it out.

00:10:11.949 --> 00:10:16.379
Like, taste--got a taste of a bunch of the other ones, and really figured out what really clicked with me.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.589
So, if there's a key takeaway,

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:26.040
the core goal of when you're picking a language or picking where to start is just find the language

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:30.060
that gets you in the door. And then learn all the core

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:37.380
fundamentals of that language. So, learn the core fundamentals of a language and learn computer science through that language. And then,

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:42.689
once you have that core base foundation, you can kind of translate it to any language,

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:46.499
any platform, or literally any industry in the future. So just

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:52.589
find that gateway drug and then you'll be addicted forever. Just like gateway drugs

00:10:52.589 --> 00:10:57.659
make you addicted to drugs, the gateway programming language makes you addicted to programming.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:03.660
So that's the end of this one. I'll create the next one in about a month or so, and I'll see you then.

